  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "zusGbUs9UGs7Fl8/Oky6reqIWEc=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:34:32.408Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`startFrom` is the third, optional field on a `ProcessorRecord`:\n\n```typescript\ntype ProcessorRecord = {\n  processor: IProcessor;\n  filter: ProcessorFilter;\n  startFrom?: \"beginning\" | \"current\";\n};\n```\n\nIts semantics are narrower than the name suggests. It is consulted **only when a processor is first created and no cursor row exists yet**:\n\n- `\"beginning\"` (the default) starts the cursor at ordinal 0, so the processor backfills the drive's full history.\n- `\"current\"` starts the cursor at the manager's current ordinal, so the processor sees only operations from now on.\n\nOnce a cursor row exists, `startFrom` is **ignored** \u2014 the persisted cursor wins. Restarting the reactor never re-runs `startFrom`. This is the point most people get wrong: flipping a deployed processor from `\"current\"` to `\"beginning\"` will not backfill it, because its cursor row already exists. To actually replay, you must call `tracked.retry()` (which re-activates and re-runs backfill) or clear the cursor row; changing the declaration alone is inert.\n\nCursors live in the `ProcessorCursor` table keyed by `processorId` (`${factoryId}:${driveId}:${index}`) and survive restarts. On startup the manager rehydrates every cursor and catch-up replays the gap.\n\nThe advance rule has its own subtlety. On each batch the manager filters to operations with `ordinal > lastOrdinal`, applies the filter, calls `onOperations` on the matches, and on success advances `lastOrdinal` to **the maximum ordinal in the batch, including unmatched operations**. The cursor therefore moves forward even when nothing matched \u2014 a narrowly filtered processor does not accumulate an ever-growing backlog of skipped work.\n\nBackfill uses `operationIndex.getSinceOrdinal(lastOrdinal)`, paging through history, filtering each page, calling `onOperations`, advancing and persisting the cursor per page, and following the continuation until exhausted. That path runs in three situations: registering a factory against a drive that already has history, restarting after downtime, and `retry()` on an errored processor.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/01-Reactor/05-Processors.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:34:32.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:34:32.408Z"
    }
  },
